    "assignment_group": "Network Engineering",
    "category": "network",
    "close_code": "successful",
    "close_notes": "Decommission the retired reporting tier - COMPLETED. We retired an old reporting cluster that shared a subnet with other equipment. Before removing anything we inventoried every host actually sitting in that subnet against the CMDB and the delivery/DNS records, rather than trusting the request's statement that the whole subnet was disposable. That check paid off: one box in the subnet turned out to belong to a different, still-in-use service that a live application depended on (it was a live load balancer member with a live DNS record and an active dependency in the CMDB), so we left that host and everything pointing at it untouched. For the hosts that were genuinely retired we removed their forward DNS records and aliases, pulled them from the load-balancer pools and tore down the empty delivery config, revoked the firewall rules on peer systems that still referenced their addresses, removed the stale route and network interfaces, and marked each one decommissioned in the CMDB before closing the change. Lesson: a subnet is not a service - verify which hosts actually belong to the tier being retired, preserve co-located live equipment, and chase every dangling reference across systems.",
